policies.statement:
// Reading an order is always allowed for an authenticated caller.
//
// The engine denies by default — a tool call with no matching permit is refused
// with "No policy applies to the request (denied by default)". So every tool the
// agent is supposed to use needs a permit here, or it stops working.
//
// `arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:324673385933:gateway/csagent-csagent-dev-tools-bppyia5tzx` is substituted by `scripts/build_policies.py` before deploy.
// The service leaves no choice about naming the gateway: an unconstrained
// `resource` is rejected ("a wildcard resource was detected"), and so is the
// resource *type* `resource is AgentCore::Gateway` ("please constrain the
// resource to a specific AgentCore::Gateway resource when creating tool-specific
// policies"). Since the ARN contains the account id, which must not be committed
// (CLAUDE.md §6), these files are templates and the ARN is rendered at build time
// from the deployed stack's output.
permit (
  principal,
  action == AgentCore::Action::"orders___get_order",
  resource == AgentCore::Gateway::"arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:324673385933:gateway/csagent-csagent-dev-tools-bppyia5tzx"
);

// Reading a customer record is always allowed for an authenticated caller.
// See permit_get_order.cedar for why the gateway ARN is a `arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:324673385933:gateway/csagent-csagent-dev-tools-bppyia5tzx`
// placeholder rather than a literal.
permit (
  principal,
  action == AgentCore::Action::"customers___get_customer",
  resource == AgentCore::Gateway::"arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:324673385933:gateway/csagent-csagent-dev-tools-bppyia5tzx"
);

// A refund is allowed only up to the $1,000 ceiling.
//
// `context.input` holds the tool call's arguments as the model produced them, so
// the amount evaluated here is the amount that would actually be refunded — not
// something the model asserts separately. The check happens at the Gateway,
// before the Lambda is invoked at all.
//
// 100000 is US cents. The tool schema declares amount_cents as an integer, so
// there is no unit ambiguity and no float comparison.
permit (
  principal,
  action == AgentCore::Action::"refunds___process_refund",
  resource == AgentCore::Gateway::"arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:324673385933:gateway/csagent-csagent-dev-tools-bppyia5tzx"
)
when { context.input.amount_cents <= 100000 };

// Refunds above $1,000 are forbidden outright.
//
// This looks redundant next to refund_limit.cedar: that permit only covers
// amounts <= 100000, and the engine denies by default, so an over-limit call is
// already refused today. The redundancy is the point.
//
// A permit-only design denies the over-limit case *by omission*. The day someone
// adds a broad `permit (principal, action, resource)` — while debugging, or to
// onboard a fourth tool in a hurry — the omission disappears and $5,000 refunds
// start succeeding, with no change to any file that mentions refunds.
//
// In Cedar an explicit forbid always beats any permit and cannot be overridden.
// So this statement, and not the absence of a permit, is what actually holds the
// ceiling. The two together mean the limit survives careless edits elsewhere.
//
// Amounts are in US cents; 100000 is $1,000.
forbid (
  principal,
  action == AgentCore::Action::"refunds___process_refund",
  resource == AgentCore::Gateway::"arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:324673385933:gateway/csagent-csagent-dev-tools-bppyia5tzx"
)
when { context.input.amount_cents > 100000 };
